---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const projCfg = {
  preset: 'projectile',
  params: [
    { name: 'u', label: 'launch speed u', min: 5, max: 40, step: 1, value: 20, unit: 'm/s' },
    { name: 'theta', label: 'launch angle θ', min: 10, max: 80, step: 1, value: 45, unit: '°' },
    { name: 'g', label: 'gravity g', min: 1.6, max: 25, step: 0.1, value: 9.8, unit: 'm/s²' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/projectile-and-2d-motion">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The trick that unlocks all two-dimensional motion: <strong>horizontal and vertical motion are
    independent</strong>. A bullet fired horizontally and a bullet dropped from the same height
    hit the ground at the same instant — gravity only edits the vertical account. A projectile is
    therefore two easy problems wearing one costume: constant velocity sideways, constant
    acceleration downward.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="launch it — set gravity to the Moon's 1.6 and watch the same throw become a monster" widget="sliderLab" config={projCfg} />

  <p>
    Three things to discover with the sliders. <strong>Range is maximised at 45°</strong> on level
    ground (any higher buys height, any lower wastes speed). <strong>Complementary angles</strong>
    (30° and 60°) give the same range by different routes. And lowering g stretches everything:
    the Moon's gravity turns a 20 m/s throw into a 250-metre punt.
  </p>

  <h2>The working equations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Horizontal:</strong> x = u·cosθ · t — no acceleration, uniform motion.</li>
    <li><strong>Vertical:</strong> y = u·sinθ · t − ½gt² — free fall from an initial upward speed.</li>
    <li><strong>Range and height:</strong> R = u²sin2θ/g and H = u²sin²θ/(2g) — both read straight off the widget's readouts in the motion-1d lesson.</li>
    <li><strong>Real air changes everything:</strong> a cricket ball's optimum angle is closer to 35°. The parabola is the vacuum answer — still the right first model.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Galileo, Two New Sciences (1638); OpenStax, College Physics (Motion in Two Dimensions).</p>
</TopicPage>
